msgid "For example, extend `WithId.AutoUUIDv7` to use generated UUIDv7 identifiers:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The entity inherits an `id` field of type `java.util.UUID`. When the entity is persisted, Hibernate automatically\n"
"generates a time-sortable version 7 UUID for this field. You do not need to declare or assign the identifier yourself.\n"
"Implementing `ManagedEntity.CustomId` also provides managed entity operations such as `persist()` and `delete()`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also provide your own database identifier explicitly and implement the `ManagedEntity.CustomId` interface in\n"
"your entity, as well as use the `ManagedRepository.CustomId` interface for your repository, to specify the database\n"
"identifier type:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You do not have to extend an identifier supertype if you define your own identifier. To combine a generated identifier\n"
"superclass with managed or record operations, implement the corresponding `CustomId` entity interface and extend the\n"
"corresponding `CustomId<Entity, Id>` repository interface; see <<entity-and-repository-type-matrix,the type matrix>>."
msgstr ""

msgid "Repositories"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A Quarkus Data Hibernate repository is an interface containing annotated finder, query, or lifecycle methods.\n"
"The annotation processor generates its implementation. A repository may also declare unannotated `default` methods\n"
"for custom operations."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A repository does not need to extend another interface, but Quarkus Data provides a few repository supertypes worth\n"
"considering for convenience. The usual conventions are to nest the repository in its entity and extend\n"
"`ManagedRepository<Entity>` for managed entities, or `RecordRepository<Entity>` for record entities. These supertypes\n"
"provide ready-made persistence and query operations in addition to your declared methods."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The examples use `jakarta.data.repository.Find` and `jakarta.data.repository.Query`.\n"
"For the behavioral differences between managed and record entities, see\n"
"<<session-and-access-models,Session and access models>>. For standalone repositories and entities without Quarkus Data\n"
"supertypes, see <<alternative-repository-definitions,Advanced usage>>."
msgstr ""

msgid "Generated finder and query methods"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can use `@Find` methods to find single instances or collections of entities by using the parameters of the method\n"
"to build a query. The Hibernate documentation contains the complete\n"
"link:https://docs.hibernate.org/orm/{hibernate-orm-version-major-minor}/introduction/html_single/#generated-finder-methods[generated finder method reference]."
msgstr ""

#, fuzzy
msgid "Finder methods may return collections of entities"
msgstr "Os métodos do Finder podem retornar coleções de entidades"

#, fuzzy
msgid "Or generate `COUNT` queries"
msgstr "Ou gerar consultas `COUNT`"

#, fuzzy
msgid "Or single entities, and any number of parameters which must all match the queried entities."
msgstr "Ou entidades únicas e qualquer número de parâmetros, os quais devem corresponder, em sua totalidade, às entidades consultadas."

msgid ""
"Use `@Delete` instead of `@Find` to delete entities matching the method parameters. The method can return the number\n"
"of deleted entities:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Alternatively, use Jakarta Data's `@Query` for entity queries or Jakarta Persistence's\n"
"`@jakarta.persistence.query.NativeQuery` for native SQL. Jakarta Data's `@Query` supports JPQL and its portable\n"
"Jakarta Data Query Language subset (JDQL). Hibernate also accepts HQL extensions in `@Query`. The Hibernate documentation contains the complete\n"
"link:https://docs.hibernate.org/orm/{hibernate-orm-version-major-minor}/introduction/html_single/#generated-query-methods[generated query method reference]."
msgstr ""

#, fuzzy
msgid "Query methods may return collections of entities"
msgstr "Os métodos de consulta podem retornar coleções de entidades"

#, fuzzy
msgid "Or projections of a single column, even multiple columns with `Object[]` and `List<Object[]>` types"
msgstr "Ou projeções de uma única coluna, ou mesmo de várias colunas com os tipos `Object[]` e `List<Object[]>`"

#, fuzzy
msgid "Queries can of course refer to method parameters."
msgstr "É claro que as consultas podem se referir a parâmetros de método."

msgid "Native queries refer to database tables and columns instead of entity names and fields."
msgstr ""

msgid "`@Query` also supports bulk `UPDATE` and `DELETE` queries. These methods can return the number of affected entities:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Generated finder and HQL query methods are type-checked at build time. They are validated for entity names, fields,\n"
"HQL syntax, and parameter names. Native SQL is validated by the database when the query is executed; see the\n"
"xref:quarkus-data-hibernate-sql.adoc[native SQL tutorial]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Obtaining and using a repository"
msgstr ""

msgid "Repositories are CDI beans and can be injected where you intend to use them:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also access a nested repository by using a generated static method on the\n"
"link:https://docs.hibernate.org/orm/{hibernate-orm-version-major-minor}/introduction/html_single/#static-metamodel[generated metamodel]\n"
"of your entity:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"All repositories defined as nested interfaces of an entity are available under their generated metamodel class through\n"
"accessor methods with the same name as the repository. This is equivalent to injecting the repository type and uses CDI\n"
"under the hood to look up the repository."
msgstr ""

msgid "The <code>ManagedRepository</code> supertype"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `ManagedRepository` type provides most of the operations needed to work with an entity, such as `persist()`,\n"
"`delete()`, `isPersistent()`, and `listAll()`."
msgstr ""

